  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-211c862c59694470": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-485",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The chains, and the bracelets, and the mufflers."
      },
      "c-631ceda4ddee4405": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-488",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The glasses, and the fine linen, and the hoods, and the wails."
      },
      "c-9600539d18edc897": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-490",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Sitting on the ground was a posture which denoted deep distress. When Job’s friends came to sympathize with him, “they sat down with him upon the ground seven days and seven nights, and none spake a word unto him: for they saw that his grief was very great” (Job 2:13). When the J…"
      },
      "c-98e7404f0f378c49": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-484",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Their cauls and their round tires like the moon."
      },
      "c-99620be0b8005118": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-489",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "See note on 1 Peter 3:3 (#886). On the other hand, baldness is considered a great calamity and is made an occasion for contempt. See note on 2 Kings 2:23 (#328). Thus the change from “well-set hair” to “baldness” would be regarded as a serious misfortune."
      },
      "c-d546fd3e507a4047": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-487",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The changeable suits of apparel, and the mantles, and the wimples, and the crisping pins."
      },
      "c-db6dd20a14e8a923": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-483",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Because the daughters of Zion are haughty, and walk with stretched-forth necks and wanton eyes, walking and mincing as they go, and making a tinkling with their feet."
      },
      "c-f5908e1f2fedd791": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-486",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Roberts, however, disputes this interpretation, and thinks these “houses of the soul” find their counterpart in certain ornaments which are worn by Hindu women, and made of silver or gold, and richly adorned with precious stones. He says: “The dancing-girls, the wives of the pand…"
      }
